Custom Rules in Bob are user-defined instructions that shape how Bob behaves — its coding style, conventions, tone, constraints, and workflow preferences — on a per-project or global basis. They are stored in a .bob/rules/ directory as Markdown (.md) files and are automatically loaded into Bob's context.

How Custom Rules Work
Location	Scope
.bob/rules/*.md	Project-level — applies to this workspace only
~/.bob/rules/*.md	Global — applies across all workspaces
Each .md file in .bob/rules/ is a freeform Markdown document. Bob reads all of them and follows their instructions throughout the conversation.

Examples of Custom Rules
1. Code Style Rule — .bob/rules/code-style.md
# Code Style

- Always use TypeScript strict mode (`"strict": true`).
- Use `const` over `let` wherever possible.
- Prefer named exports over default exports.
- All functions must have explicit return types.
- Use 2-space indentation; never tabs.

2. Git Commit Convention Rule — .bob/rules/git.md
# Git Conventions

- Commit messages must follow Conventional Commits format:
  `feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `test:`
- Never commit directly to `main` — always create a feature branch.
- PR titles must reference a JIRA ticket, e.g. `feat(AUTH-123): add OAuth login`.

3. Testing Rule — .bob/rules/testing.md
# Testing Standards

- All new functions must have at least one unit test.
- Use Vitest for all tests in this project.
- Test files live alongside source files: `foo.ts` → `foo.test.ts`.
- Mock all external HTTP calls — never hit real APIs in tests.

4. Architecture Rule — .bob/rules/architecture.md
# Architecture Constraints

- This project follows a layered architecture: Controller → Service → Repository.
- Business logic must never live in controllers.
- Database access is only allowed inside `src/repositories/`.
- Do not add new npm dependencies without explicit user approval.

5. Language / Communication Rule — .bob/rules/communication.md
# Communication

- Always respond in British English.
- Keep explanations concise — prefer bullet points over paragraphs.
- When suggesting a refactor, always explain the reason in one sentence.

6. Security Rule — .bob/rules/security.md
# Security

- Never log sensitive fields: `password`, `token`, `secret`, `apiKey`.
- All user input must be validated with Zod before use.
- Secrets are loaded from environment variables only — never hard-coded.

Creating Your First Rule
Just create the directory and drop in a Markdown file:

New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path .bob/rules -Force
New-Item -Path .bob/rules/code-style.md

Bob will pick up the rules automatically in the next conversation turn. You can have as many rule files as you like — splitting them by concern (style, git, testing, security) keeps them easy to maintain.

